Usage: `<CheckoutScreen items={cartItems} onBack={() => navigate("cart")} onPay={(address, items) => api.pay(address, items)} />`.

Key decisions, per Telegram Mini App conventions: Back is handled by Telegram's `BackButton` (shown/hidden by this screen, routed to your own `onBack`) rather than an in-page arrow; Pay is handled by Telegram's `MainButton` (text, enabled state, and progress spinner all driven from form validity/submit state) rather than a sticky footer button; colors come entirely from `--tg-theme-*` CSS variables so the screen matches the user's theme and reacts live to light/dark switches; layout uses `--tg-viewport-stable-height` and both safe-area inset variables instead of `100vh`. A plain `<button>` fallback for Back/Pay only renders when `window.Telegram.WebApp` isn't present (e.g. a regular browser), so the component still works standalone.
